{"hash":"fda237a2f97c3fdc","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":90.305,"said":"Here, beside this small spring, the temple trust tells the story in its own way, the way this village has told it for generations. Long ago, it says, the demon Tripurasura did penance in this very forest, and won from Shiva the gift of near-immortality, on condition that he use it for the good of the world. He forgot his promise and began to torment everyone. So Shiva asked Parvati to stand with him. She is worshipped up on the ridge as Kamalaja Devi, because she was honoured with lotus flowers. Together they took one form, half Shiva and half Shakti, Ardhanarishvara, and on the full moon of Kartik they destroyed Tripurasura. Afterwards, as Shiva sat here to rest, sweat streamed from his body, and from those streams, the trust says, the river Bhima began. This is Kusharanya Tirtha, where she sets out eastward on her long journey. And the demon’s wives, Dakini and Shakini, came to Shiva for refuge. He blessed them, and promised that their names would come before his own. So the hymn calls this place ‘Dakinyam Bhimashankaram’. Now walk round to the front of the temple. A great bell is waiting there.\n\nDid you know? Kartik Purnima is kept here as Tripurari Purnima, the night Tripurasura fell, with a maha-aarti and the palanquins of Bhimashankar and Kamalaja Devi carried in procession.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1114},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1130,"end":1301}],"words":[[0.098,0.397,0,4],[0.759,0.457,6,12],[1.228,0.144,13,17],[1.384,0.361,18,23],[1.757,0.469,24,30],[2.466,0.108,32,35],[2.586,0.421,36,42],[3.019,0.325,43,48],[3.355,0.3,49,54],[3.668,0.096,55,58],[3.776,0.481,59,64],[4.269,0.144,65,67],[4.425,0.216,68,71],[4.653,0.24,72,75],[4.906,0.288,76,79],[5.459,0.12,81,84],[5.591,0.192,85,88],[5.795,0.264,89,93],[6.072,0.312,94,101],[6.396,0.228,102,105],[6.637,0.288,106,110],[6.937,0.12,111,113],[7.069,0.156,114,117],[7.237,0.757,118,129],[8.882,0.